# Mutation cases for §scxml-C-2 — a form-encoded param carries the value, not
# the sender's language.
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/wire_value_is_not_engine_source.cases`.
#
# An HTTP `<param>`, a `targetexpr` and a `delayexpr` leave the process for a
# reader that is not an engine at all. Until 2026-08-21 the function that
# rendered them was the engine's own literal renderer — Lua's spelling, living
# on the engine-neutral value type — so the same param read `nil` from the Rust
# backend and empty from the C++ one, `{1, 2}` here and `[1,2]` there.
# Measured that day: six backends, six answers, one field.
#
# The engine-source direction that renderer also served is gone as of the same
# week: §scxml-6.4.3 asks for the VALUE of an `<invoke>` `<param>`, and
# rendering it as source lost every value Lua cannot spell. Its case here went
# with it, and `integration_resources/invoke_param_seeds_declared_child_data/`
# is the witness that replaced it — on seven channels rather than one.
#
# What each case restores, and what it would cost unnoticed:
#   * absence as a word — a peer receives the four letters `nil` where the
#     document sent nothing, and cannot tell it from a value.
#   * a number with Rust's tail — `5.0` where ECMAScript's `String(5)` is `5`,
#     so a receiver comparing the text against "5" reads false.
#   * a string re-quoted — the quotes the old path added and then trimmed off,
#     which ate the quotes a value carried itself.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is
# reported INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-rust-runtime --test wire_value_is_not_engine_source
mutation_targets backends/rust/runtime/src/helpers/event_data.rs
mutation_oracles backends/rust/runtime/tests/wire_value_is_not_engine_source.rs

mutation_case "the wire spells absence with Lua's word again" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/helpers/event_data.rs",
     """        ScriptValue::Null | ScriptValue::Undefined => String::new(),""",
     """        ScriptValue::Null | ScriptValue::Undefined => "nil".to_string(),""")
PY

mutation_case "the wire gives an integral number a decimal tail again" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/helpers/event_data.rs",
     """            } else if f.fract() == 0.0 && f.abs() < 1e15 {
                // ECMAScript `String(5)` is "5". A `.0` tail is the Rust
                // spelling of the number, not the document's.
                format!("{}", *f as i64)""",
     """            } else if f.fract() == 0.0 && f.abs() < 1e15 {
                format!("{:.1}", f)""")
PY

mutation_case "the wire quotes a string that was already text again" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/helpers/event_data.rs",
     """        ScriptValue::String(s) => s.clone(),
        // The receiving end reads XML from document text (§scxml-B-2), so a
        // DOM crosses as that text rather than as a quoted JSON string.""",
     """        ScriptValue::String(s) => format!("\\"{}\\"", escape_json_string(s)),
        // The receiving end reads XML from document text (§scxml-B-2), so a
        // DOM crosses as that text rather than as a quoted JSON string.""")
PY

# ⚠ Not a case any more: "the engine's literal answers in someone else's
# grammar", which mutated `lua_literal`'s absence arm from `nil` to `null`.
# That renderer no longer exists — the invoke path passes values, so nothing
# asked an engine to spell one, and a required trait method with no caller is
# a tax rather than a contract. The clause it guarded is now held by
# `invoke_param_seeds_declared_child_data`, whose `infinite` phase is red on
# any channel that renders a value as source.
